it was supposed to be a weekend of  remembrance and relaxation Filipinos  traditionally flocked to cemeteries this  time of year to remember deceased  friends and families but this Cemetery  the largest in Manila was closed as the  threat of tropical storm gnologue  flower vendors were willing to risk it  but there was no business  there was a pandemic right we really  wanted to see how many people would have  come here for the first time in two  years  we're staying put because we have to  guard the flowers  bus terminals that would have been  crowded were nearly empty many of those  who did decide to travel got stuck in  ports with air and sea travel also  suspended  to boost tourism the government has  declared this long weekend a holiday a  tropical storm Nagi has hampered travel  plans in the affected areas worse rescue  operations are in full swing as entire  Villages are submerged by flood water  before the storm had even made landfall  in the Philippines parts of the country  were already experiencing heavy downpour  for days flash floods caught many in the  province of makinda Now by surprise  rescue workers scrambled to get to those  who needed help but help did not come  quickly enough for one Village kushong  instantly became a site of past casualty  and the aftermath was nothing short of  catastrophic Forum chief minister ahud  at an emergency meeting on Saturday  president Ferdinand Marcos wondered how  such a tragedy could have happened  what caused the flooding why weren't  they evacuated what happened and why so  many deaths  government officials hope the death toll  won't increase significantly but the  storm size covered nearly the entire  country  and as it continued to sweep through the  main island of Luzon through the Sunday  it's difficult to say how much more  Devastation it will leave in its wake  me below Al Jazeera Manila
 

